= {{id name="HowtoinstallXWiki-Accessingyourwiki"/}}Accessing your wiki =

Once you've installed XWiki using one of the methods defined above, you can access it by pointing your web browser to {{code language="none"}}http://localhost:8080/xwiki{{/code}}.

{{attachments/}}

= {{id name="HowtoinstallXWiki-Troubleshooting"/}}Troubleshooting =

You can verify some basic settings of your XWiki installation (on Tomcat, MySQL) using the [[Admin Tools application>>url:https://extensions.xwiki.org/xwiki/bin/view/Extension/Admin%20Tools%20Application||shape="rect"]]. Keep in mind that some of these tools only work in a Linux environment.

== {{id name="HowtoinstallXWiki-WhattodowithanemptyXWiki?"/}}What to do with an empty XWiki? ==

== {{id name="HowtoinstallXWiki-RunningmultipleinstancesofXWikiinthesamecontainer"/}}Running multiple instances of XWiki in the same container ==

If you get this error:

{{code}}
com.xpn.xwiki.XWikiException: Error number 3 in 0: Could not initialize main XWiki context

Wrapped Exception: Failed to get cache factory component

Caused by:

org.infinispan.jmx.JmxDomainConflictException: Domain already registered org.xwiki.infinispan
{{/code}}

You need to edit {{code language="none"}}WEB-INF/cache/infinispan/config.xml{{/code}} for each instance of XWiki.

(% start="1" %)
1. For Infinispan up till version 6, change the {{code language="none"}}jmxDomain{{/code}} value (found under the {{code language="none"}}globalJmxStatistics{{/code}} tag) to have a unique name, or add  {{code language="none"}}allowDuplicateDomains="true"{{/code}} attribute for {{code language="none"}}globalJmxStatistics{{/code}} element.
1. For Infinispan version 7, add {{code language="none"}}&lt;jmx duplicate-domains="true"/>{{/code}} element in {{code language="none"}}&lt;cache-container...{{/code}} element.
           

== {{id name="HowtoinstallXWiki-RunningXWikibehindaproxyserver"/}}Running XWiki behind a proxy server ==

The extension manager relies on remote repositories for finding and fetching available extensions.  If your XWiki is installed in a network which requires accessing the internet through a proxy server, most likely searching for extensions will fail and log files will contain {{code language="none"}}connection refused{{/code}} exceptions.

In such cases, you can configure XWiki to use the proxy server.

XWiki uses default Java proxy configuration through Apache httpclient. See the [[Java Networking and Proxies documentation>>url:http://docs.oracle.com/javase/6/docs/technotes/guides/net/proxies.html||shape="rect"]] for more details.
The main idea is to add a set of proxy-related properties to the system scope.

For an installation using Tomcat 6 it could be done in the following way: modify {{code language="none"}}/&lt;tomcat-home>/bin/catalina.sh{{/code}} and added proxies to the {{code language="none"}}JAVA_OPTS{{/code}} variable definition:

{{code}}
JAVA_OPTS="$JAVA_OPTS -Dappserver.home=$CATALINA_HOME -Dappserver.base=$CATALINA_HOME -Dhttp.proxyHost=proxy.mycompany.com -Dhttp.proxyPort=7777 -Dhttps.proxyHost=proxy.mycompany.com -Dhttps.proxyPort=7777 -Xmx512m -XX:MaxPermSize=196m $XWIKI_OPTS"
{{/code}}

For an installation of the HSQLDB demo package you could do it by adding something like this to {{code language="none"}}/xwiki-platform-distribution-flavor-jetty-hsqldb-x.y/start_xwiki.sh{{/code}}:

{{code}}
XWIKI_OPTS="$XWIKI_OPTS -Dhttp.proxyHost=proxy.mycompany.com -Dhttp.proxyPort=7777 -Dhttps.proxyHost=proxy.mycompany.com -Dhttps.proxyPort=7777"
{{/code}}

Note: If your proxy server only allows partial access to the internet you need to make sure that the sites {{code language="none"}}store.xwiki.com{{/code}}, {{code language="none"}}extensions.xwiki.org{{/code}} and {{code language="none"}}nexus.xwiki.org{{/code}} (note that one is .{{code language="none"}}com{{/code}} and the others are {{code language="none"}}.org{{/code}}) can be accessed.

If your proxy server require authentication you can use {{code language="none"}}-Dhttps.proxyUser{{/code}} and {{code language="none"}}-Dhttps.proxyPassword{{/code}} (and the http alteratives).

=== {{id name="HowtoinstallXWiki-Debuggingproxyconfigurationissues"/}}Debugging proxy configuration issues ===

If you are having trouble with debugging whether or not the proxy configuration you've just made is properly read by XWiki, you could drop the following groovy script in a new page on your XWiki instance and look for the values of the (proxy) properties you're trying to change:

{{code}}
{{
}}

"== Environment Variables =="

getenv
().
each
{
,
->
"Name: {{{$name}}} = Value {{{$value}}}"
}

""

"== Java System Properties =="

getProperties
().
each
{
,
->
"Name: {{{$name}}} = Value {{{$value}}}"
}

{{/
}}
{{/code}}

Additionally, you could also include this test content that actually tests if your XWiki instance is able to access the Internet (using the "httpbin" service; you can use anything else, including [[google.com>>url:http://google.com||shape="rect"]]), for both HTTP and HTTPs:

{{code}}


{{

xwiki
getURLContent
"http://httpbin.org/ip"

{{




{{

xwiki
getURLContent
"https://httpbin.org/ip"

{{
{{/code}}

== {{id name="HowtoinstallXWiki-Unsupportedmajor.minorversion51.0"/}}Unsupported major.minor version 51.0 ==

This is because starting with version 6.0, XWiki requires Java 7 and you're using Java 6. You need to upgrade your Java version.

== {{id name="HowtoinstallXWiki-Installingwithoutinternetconnection"/}}Installing without internet connection ==

=== {{id name="HowtoinstallXWiki-XIPPackage"/}}XIP Package ===

Since 9.5 A XIP package is offered containing an offline extension repository with all the extensions making the Standard Flavor.

This is actually a zip file containing the required XWiki extensions for both the main wiki and subwikis, in the same format as the one of the local extensions repository. It allows the [[Distribution Wizard>>url:https://platform.xwiki.org/xwiki/bin/view/Features/DistributionWizard||shape="rect"]] to find the extensions locally instead of getting them online and is thus suited for offline installations of XWiki.

* (((
[[download>>url:https://www.xwiki.org/xwiki/bin/view/Main/Download||shape="rect"]]

 it (use the exact same version of the XIP package as the version of XWiki you have)
)))
* Unzip it (rename it to {{code language="none"}}.zip{{/code}} if your zip tool does not recognize it) in the folder {{code language="none"}}&lt;permanentdirectory>/extension/repository{{/code}} (if you get complains about already existing files don't overwrite them)
* Make sure the user running XWiki has the permissions to write to these unzipped files
* Restart XWiki if it was running
* Resume standard installation, this time it will find the flavor locally

=== {{id name="HowtoinstallXWiki-Othermethods"/}}Other methods ===

Several options:

* Set up a local Extension Repository, either as a Maven Repository or using the [[Extension Repository Application>>url:https://extensions.xwiki.org/xwiki/bin/view/Extension/Repository%20Application||shape="rect"]]. Of course you'll need to [[prefill it with all the extensions you'll wish to install when offline>>url:https://www.xwiki.org/xwiki/bin/view/Documentation/AdminGuide/FillLocalMavenRepository||shape="rect"]], which isn't something easy to do.
* (((
[[Install the Standalone Distribution>>url:https://www.xwiki.org/xwiki/bin/view/Documentation/AdminGuide/InstallationStandalone||shape="rect"]]

 which contains a default set of wiki pages.
)))
* Use the [[Offline Packager Maven plugin>>url:https://github.com/xwiki-contrib/maven-plugin-offline-xwiki-repository-packager||shape="rect"]].

=== {{id name="HowtoinstallXWiki-ExtensionRepositories"/}}Extension Repositories ===

XWiki performs some checks at startup and even at runtime and tries to connects to remote Extension repositories. This will not cause any problem if you're offline but you'll get some error logs. You may want to disable Extension repositories by editing your {{code language="none"}}xwiki.properties{{/code}} config file and adding the following empty property (make sure that this is the only property defined with the name):

{{code}}
extension.repositories=
{{/code}}

=== {{id name="HowtoinstallXWiki-Deprecatedmethods"/}}Deprecated methods ===

==== {{id name="HowtoinstallXWiki-ManuallyImportWikiPages"/}}Manually Import Wiki Pages ====

This is not supported anymore since 9.5

You can also skip the Distribution Wizard import the default Wiki that we are providing as a [[XAR file>>url:http://download.forge.ow2.org/xwiki/||shape="rect"]] by using the [[Import feature>>url:https://platform.xwiki.org/xwiki/bin/view/AdminGuide/ImportExport||shape="rect"]]. Since there's no Admin user created at this point, you'll need to [[log in as >>url:https://platform.xwiki.org/xwiki/bin/view/AdminGuide/Configuration#HEnablesuperadminaccount||shape="rect"]]{{code language="none"}}superadmin{{/code}} to be able to perform this import. 

If you're upgrading an existing wiki using the manual Import feature, be careful that the XAR import operation will replace the content of the existing pages of your wiki with the content of the XAR (New pages that you have created will not be modified). As a consequence it's critical that you [[perform a backup>>url:https://platform.xwiki.org/xwiki/bin/view/AdminGuide/Backup||shape="rect"]] first and read the upgrade nodes bellow carefully.

On the other hand, if you use the Distribution Wizard, it'll automatically perform a merge and prompt you for action in case there's a conflict it cannot resolve automatically. Thus using the Distribution Wizard is what we recommend.

== {{id name="HowtoinstallXWiki-GettinganerroraboutSSLcertificate"/}}Getting an error about SSL certificate ==

If you get an error in the log telling you that it fail to access some some https website it usually means you have a too old version of Java 8 (older that update 101) and you should upgrade it (does not need to be upgraded to next major version of Java, just make sure you have at least Java 8 update 101).

{{code/}}

== {{id name="HowtoinstallXWiki-"Filepathtoolong"onWindowswhenunzipping"/}}"File path too long" on Windows when unzipping ==

The Windows Operating System imposes a [[file path limit to around 260 characters>>url:https://msdn.microsoft.com/en-us/library/aa365247(VS.85).aspx#maxpath||shape="rect"]]. XWiki's zip file can, in some cases, reach paths as long a 182 characters, or even longer. To avoid such errors you could do one of the following:

* Install in a folder that is as close to the root of the drive as possible and uses a small name, e.g. {{code language="none"}}C:\xwiki{{/code}}
* Install XWiki using the [[cygwin>>url:https://www.cygwin.com/||shape="rect"]] environment that overcomes this limitation
* Starting with Windows 10, you can set or create the {{code language="none"}}EnableLongPaths{{/code}} with a value of {{code language="none"}}1{{/code}} in the in the {{code language="none"}}HKEY_LOCAL_MACHINE\SYSTEM\CurrentControlSet\Control\FileSystem{{/code}} Windows Registry in order to activate support for long paths. More details and step-by-step instructions are available in various online tutorials such as [[this one>>url:https://www.howtogeek.com/266621/how-to-make-windows-10-accept-file-paths-over-260-characters/||shape="rect"]].
* As Windows Unarchiver cannot unzip files with long paths, [[7-Zip>>url:https://www.7-zip.org/||shape="rect"]]can be used instead.</string>
