          <wikiDocumentRevision revision="1">
            <p>
              <parameters>
                <entry>
                  <string>revision_author</string>
                  <string>XWiki.5be2ea7a421c5f44fc455eec</string>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <string>revision_date</string>
                  <date>2023-02-04 09:46:07.614 UTC</date>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <string>title</string>
                  <string>The Singer Building</string>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <string>content</string>
                  <string>{{toc/}}

= {{id name="TheSingerBuilding-Headingno1"/}}Heading no 1 =

The **Singer Building** (also known as the **Singer Tower**)[[~[a~]&gt;&gt;url:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Singer_Building#cite_note-3||shape="rect"]] was an office building and [[early skyscraper>>url:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Early_skyscrapers||shape="rect"]] in [[Manhattan>>url:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Manhattan||shape="rect"]], New York City. The headquarters of the [[Singer Manufacturing Company>>url:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Singer_Corporation||shape="rect"]], it was at the northwestern corner of [[Liberty Street>>url:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Liberty_Street_(Manhattan)||shape="rect"]] and [[Broadway>>url:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Broadway_(Manhattan)||shape="rect"]] in the [[Financial District>>url:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Financial_District,_Manhattan||shape="rect"]] of [[Lower Manhattan>>url:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Lower_Manhattan||shape="rect"]]. [[Frederick Gilbert Bourne>>url:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Frederick_Gilbert_Bourne||shape="rect"]], leader of the Singer Company, commissioned the building, which architect [[Ernest Flagg>>url:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ernest_Flagg||shape="rect"]] designed in multiple phases from 1897 to 1908. The building's architecture contained elements of the [[Beaux-Arts>>url:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Beaux-Arts_architecture||shape="rect"]] and [[French Second Empire>>url:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/French_Second_Empire_style||shape="rect"]] styles.

= {{id name="TheSingerBuilding-Headingno2"/}}Heading no 2 =

The building was composed of four distinct sections. The original 10-story Singer Building at 149 Broadway was erected between 1897 and 1898, and the adjoining 14-story Bourne Building on Liberty Street was built from 1898 to 1899. In the first decade of the 20th century, the two buildings were expanded to form the 14-story base of the Singer Tower, which rose another 27 stories. The [[facade>>url:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Facade||shape="rect"]] was made of brick, stone, and [[terracotta>>url:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Architectural_terracotta||shape="rect"]]. A [[dome>>url:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Dome||shape="rect"]] with a [[lantern>>url:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Roof_lantern||shape="rect"]] capped the tower. The [[foundation>>url:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Foundation_(engineering)||shape="rect"]] of the tower was excavated using [[caissons>>url:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Caisson_(engineering)||shape="rect"]]; the building's base rested on shallower foundations. The Singer Building used a [[steel skeleton>>url:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Steel_frame||shape="rect"]], though [[load-bearing walls>>url:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Load-bearing_wall||shape="rect"]] initially supported the original structure before modification. When completed, the 41-story building had a [[marble>>url:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Marble||shape="rect"]]-[[clad>>url:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Stone_veneer||shape="rect"]] entrance lobby, 16 elevators, 410,000 square feet (38,000 m2) of office space, and an observatory.

= {{id name="TheSingerBuilding-Headingno3"/}}Heading no 3 =

With a roof height of 612 feet (187 m), the Singer Tower was the [[tallest building in the world>>url:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skyscraper#History_of_the_tallest_skyscrapers||shape="rect"]] from 1908 to 1909, when it was surpassed by the [[Metropolitan Life Insurance Company Tower>>url:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Metropolitan_Life_Insurance_Company_Tower||shape="rect"]]. The base occupied the building's entire [[land lot>>url:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Land_lot||shape="rect"]]; the tower's floors took up just one-sixth of that area. Despite being regarded as a city icon, the Singer Building was razed between 1967 and 1969 to make way for [[One Liberty Plaza>>url:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/One_Liberty_Plaza||shape="rect"]], which had several times more office space than the Singer Tower. At the time of its destruction, the Singer Building was the [[tallest building ever to be demolished>>url:https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/List_of_tallest_voluntarily_demolished_buildings||shape="rect"]].</string>
                </entry>
                <entry>
                  <string>syntax</string>
                  <org.xwiki.rendering.syntax.Syntax>
                    <type>
                      <name>XWiki</name>
                      <id>xwiki</id>
                      <variants class="empty-list"/>
                    </type>
                    <version>2.1</version>
                  </org.xwiki.rendering.syntax.Syntax>
                </entry>
              </parameters>
            </p>
          </wikiDocumentRevision>
        </wikiDocumentLocale>
      </wikiDocument>
    </wikiSpace>
